22. The narrative arc I infer from the discussion

The cleanest reconstruction of what they were trying to build is:

1 — What is UK Residential / MFG?

Market size, segmentation and addressable market.

2 — Why does it matter to us?

Issuance, franchise presence, teams/products involved and historical credentials.

3 — Who are the important players?

Market map showing specialist lenders, scale and approximate shares.

4–9 — Client reference pages

One per material current/potential originator:
business, scale, products, funding, relationship, economics and strategic value.

10 — Compare the clients

Side-by-side matrix of the most important metrics.

11 — Our current portfolio

Exposure, utilisation, maturity, returns, ROE/SVA and concentration.

12 — What opportunities have we not captured?

Declined business, waived economics, accordions, mandates not pursued.

13 — Strategic opportunity

Core lending economics + public distribution/prime RMBS fee upside.

14 — Management decision / ask

Target balance sheet, preferred client universe and principles for allocating capital.

That sequence is my synthesis of the discussion, rather than a slide order explicitly agreed verbatim. But it captures almost every substantive deck idea raised.

The single most important message

The strongest theme throughout the conversation was:

The deck should turn client selection from an anecdotal, individual-client decision into a fact-based portfolio and franchise decision.

Management should leave understanding:

how big the market is → who matters → how well positioned the bank is → what each relationship contributes → what has already been sacrificed → what balance sheet is needed → what broader fee/franchise opportunity is protected by keeping the UK Residential business.